<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Additional Experiments and Findings</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we demonstrate and investigate several aspects of our approaches.
These findings illustrate interesting properties of our approach, and we hope to have a deeper understanding on our approach and inspire future works by describing these findings.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Distortion-Perception Trade-off via Early Stopping</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we discuss an interesting finding, showing that our approach has a inherent Distortion-Perception trade-off if stopping earlier during the generation process.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To understand this, let’s recall the analytical form of <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S3.E8" title="In Inference-time scaling with sampling ‣ 3.3 Inference-Time Scaling and Control ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Compression as Adaptation:Implicit Visual Representation with Diffusion Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.
This reveals two ways to recover <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<ol id="A2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">sample <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math> and follow the denoising dynamics;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">apply the one-step map <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=x_{1}-v^{*}(x_{1},1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=x_{1}-v^{*}(x_{1},1)</annotation></semantics></math> directly.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">combine 1 and 2: run the denoising dynamics until time <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, then perform the final reconstruction in one step using <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=x_{\tau}-\tau\,v^{*}(x_{\tau},\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=x_{\tau}-\tau\,v^{*}(x_{\tau},\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> .</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">With a perfectly learned vector field, all choices recover the same <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.
In practice, however, the switching time <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> becomes a meaningful control knob, leading to a distortion-perception trade-off.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">One one hand, the perceptual realism typically improves monotonically as we switch later, since a later switch better leverages the pretrained model’s generative ability.
On the other hand, the distortion is typically non-monotonic w.r.t the switching time.
Switching too late increases distortion due to error accumulation, while switching too early also lead to higher distortion as the sample is still dominated by noise and the learned vector field has larger error.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This non-monotonic distortion trend is characterized by the following proposition, which bounds the reconstruction error w.r.t the switching time <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition B.1</span></span><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume the learned vector field <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(\cdot,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(\cdot,t)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz for any <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the approximation error of the learned vector field at time <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> along the clean path, and let <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> be the state error at time <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the final reconstruction error <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|\delta\|\leq(\tau L+1)\exp(L(1-\tau))\int_{\tau}^{1}\|e_{t}\|\mathrm{d}t+\tau\|e_{\tau}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|\delta\|\leq(\tau L+1)\exp(L(1-\tau))\int_{\tau}^{1}\|e_{t}\|\mathrm{d}t+\tau\|e_{\tau}\|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We derive this bound in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 Bounds on the Distortion of Early Stopping ‣ Appendix D Proposition and Proofs ‣ Compression as Adaptation:Implicit Visual Representation with Diffusion Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a>.
In practice, we found <math id="A2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|e_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|e_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math> increases as <math id="A2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> grows.
From this bound, when <math id="A2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the error is dominated by the integral over <math id="A2.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, while when <math id="A2.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\rightarrow 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\rightarrow 1</annotation></semantics></math>, the error directly arises from the <math id="A2.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We empirically demonstrate this trade-off on the 7 videos from UVG dataset, trained with <math id="A2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=1.5e-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1.5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=1.5e-3</annotation></semantics></math>.
We run inference with ODE discretized into 100 steps, early stop at every time step, and calculate PSNR, DISTS, LPIPS.
In <a href="#A2.F8" title="In B.1 Distortion-Perception Trade-off via Early Stopping ‣ Appendix B Additional Experiments and Findings ‣ Compression as Adaptation:Implicit Visual Representation with Diffusion Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, we visualize the metrics against the early stop time <math id="A2.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that the generation starts from <math id="A2.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math> and ends at <math id="A2.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math> when interpreting the plot.
As shown in <a href="#S4.F6" title="In 4.3 Inference-Time Scaling ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Compression as Adaptation:Implicit Visual Representation with Diffusion Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, stopping early yields an essential gain of about 1.5 dB in PSNR.
However, this comes at the expense of perceptual quality: perceptual metrics degrade noticeably, resulting in a clear distortion-perception trade-off.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.07615v1/early_stop.svg" id="A2.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/87;" width="476" height="87"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F8.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>: </span><span id="A2.F8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Distortion-Perception Trade-off via early stopping on UVG.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Distortion-Perception Trade-off via Early Stopping <em id="A2.SS2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">with Inference-time Scaling</em></h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The above conclusion and theory are observed and derived for our method without inference-time scaling.
Therefore, a natural question is whether the DP trade-off still holds when coupled with the inference-scaling approach we proposed.
We investigate this on the same UVG videos as in the last section.
We perform inference-time scaling with 1,000 steps, each with <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>10</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{10}</annotation></semantics></math> samples in <a href="#A2.F9" title="In B.2 Distortion-Perception Trade-off via Early Stopping with Inference-time Scaling ‣ Appendix B Additional Experiments and Findings ‣ Compression as Adaptation:Implicit Visual Representation with Diffusion Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.
As we can see, both distortion and perception metrics have the same trend.
And early stop still provide a trade-off between distortion and perception when <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is smaller than a certain threshold.
However, this threshold is much smaller than the case without inference-time scaling.
This is because scaling corrects the error at the early stage of denoising (<math id="A2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> close to 1), leading to a smaller error accumulation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F9" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.07615v1/early_stop_scaling.svg" id="A2.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/87;" width="476" height="87"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F9.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 9</span>: </span><span id="A2.F9.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Distortion-Perception Trade-off via early stopping coupled with inference-time scaling on UVG.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>Additional Results on Editing</h3>

<figure id="A2.F10" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.07615v1/image_res.png" id="A2.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/177;" width="476" height="177" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F10.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 10</span>: </span><span id="A2.F10.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Image editing results with one-vector representation. In the last example, we also show that image generation ability is largely maintained.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A2.F11" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.07615v1/res2.png" id="A2.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/150;" width="476" height="150" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F11.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 11</span>: </span><span id="A2.F11.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Video editing results on Wan-2.1-14B, with one vector of length 524288. In the first example, changing the dog to a panda results in a coherent edit. However, in the second example, due to biases in the base model, black hair is often associated with Asian individuals, which leads the model to modify the face accordingly.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A2.F12" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.07615v1/res.png" id="A2.F12.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/159;" width="476" height="159" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F12.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 12</span>: </span><span id="A2.F12.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Overfitting in the representation can also influence the generation under irrelevant prompts. When the prompt is highly relevant to the scene or contains key objectives, the model’s output is strongly influenced and may even directly reproduce the overfitted video. For prompts that are less relevant, the influence is weaker but still observable, affecting attributes such as color, motion, or background.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.4 </span>Trade-off between Reconstruction and Memorization</h3>

<div id="A2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond reconstruction, implicit visual representations can function as <em id="A2.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">visual memories</em> that directly influence the generation behavior of the underlying diffusion foundation model. In practice, we observe that the number of fine-tuning iterations plays a critical role in balancing faithful memorization of a given visual signal and preservation of the model’s general generative capability.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, excessive fine-tuning to overfit a single signal can substantially degrade the foundation model’s ability to generate diverse and semantically coherent content. As the adaptation increasingly memorizes the target visual instance, the model gradually loses its original generative prior, leading to a collapse in generalization. At the same time, the reconstruction (compression) quality is improved. This effect is particularly pronounced when the optimization is continued well beyond the point of reconstruction saturation.
We further find that this trade-off is strongly dependent on the capacity of the underlying foundation model. Larger and more expressive models (e.g., Qwen-Image-20B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>) exhibit higher resilience to adaptation, requiring substantially more optimization steps (often exceeding 500 iterations) before noticeable degradation of generative ability occurs. In contrast, smaller models (e.g., Wan-2.1-1.3B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wan et al., 2025</a>)</cite>) are significantly more fragile: even moderate levels of overfitting can quickly disrupt their original generation behaviour.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Additional Backgrounds and Related Works</h2>

<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Explicit + Implicit Representations for Visual Compression</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, several works have explored combining explicit and implicit representations for visual compression, aiming to balance entropy efficiency and expressive reconstruction.
For images, COOL-CHIC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ladune et al., 2023</a>)</cite> proposes a hybrid codec that explicitly models entropy with an autoregressive prior while decoding pixels using an implicit coordinate-based neural representation.
For videos, HNeRV <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2023</a>)</cite> extends the NeRV framework by hierarchically encoding video content into explicit network parameters and latent codes, while relying on an implicit neural function to reconstruct frames from continuous coordinates.
Later, NVRC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwan et al., 2024</a>)</cite> introduces a neural video representation that combines explicitly quantized latent variables for entropy coding with an implicit neural decoder for frame synthesis. These works can be regarded as a combination of explicit compression and implicit compression, which are shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F1.sf1" title="Figure 1(a) ‣ Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Compression as Adaptation:Implicit Visual Representation with Diffusion Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(a)</span></a> and <a href="#S1.F1.sf2" title="Figure 1(b) ‣ Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Compression as Adaptation:Implicit Visual Representation with Diffusion Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(b)</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>LoRA as Visual Memory</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Personalization and controllable adaptation both full-parameter and parameter-efficient fine-tuning. DreamBooth <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ruiz et al., 2023</a>)</cite> demonstrates that a diffusion model can memorize a subject or visual concept by overfitting on a small set of images, effectively treating model parameters as a form of visual memory. However, this requires updating a large portion of the model and incurs significant storage and deployment cost per concept. In the video domain, methods such as Customize-A-Video <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and VideoMage <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> show that LoRA modules can encode motion or subject-specific priors from limited examples and be reused across novel scenes, highlighting their role as transferable motion or identity memories. LoRA-Edit <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> demonstrates that LoRA can record localized, edit-specific behaviours that persist temporally while preserving the frozen backbone. Beyond single-adapter usage, contrastive test-time composition of multiple LoRAs reveals that independently trained adapters can be combined at inference to retrieve and blend multiple visual concepts, reinforcing a memory-bank interpretation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meral et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. At the system level, V-LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mi et al., 2025</a>)</cite> formalizes LoRA as an on-demand, deployable unit for large language models. Collectively, these works suggest that LoRA adapters function not merely as optimization tools, but as compact implicit visual memories that can be stored, composed, and injected into foundation models to control generation behavior.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.3 </span>Diffusion and Flow Matching</h3>

<div id="A3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide more background on diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2020</a>)</cite> and flow matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lipman et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, which are equivalent in certain cases.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Diffusion models.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion models define a forward noising SDE, corrupting data into noise:</p>
<table id="A3.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{d}x_{t}=\beta_{t}x_{t}\mathrm{d}t+\sigma_{t}\mathrm{d}\overrightarrow{w}_{t},\quad x_{0}\sim p_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>→</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{d}x_{t}=\beta_{t}x_{t}\mathrm{d}t+\sigma_{t}\mathrm{d}\overrightarrow{w}_{t},\quad x_{0}\sim p_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> hyperparameters, which are typically chosen so that the final state of this SDE follows Gaussian distribution.
For simplicity, without loss of generality, we now assume <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are chosen such that <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Assume the marginal density of the SDE at time step <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the score function is defined as <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\log p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\log p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By Nelson’s relation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nelson, 1967</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anderson, 1982</a>)</cite>, the time-reveral of the above SDE is given by</p>
<table id="A3.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{d}x_{t}=\left(\beta_{t}x_{t}-\sigma_{t}^{2}\nabla\log p_{t}(x_{t})\right)\mathrm{d}t+\sigma_{t}\mathrm{d}\overleftarrow{w}_{t},\quad x_{1}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>←</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{d}x_{t}=\left(\beta_{t}x_{t}-\sigma_{t}^{2}\nabla\log p_{t}(x_{t})\right)\mathrm{d}t+\sigma_{t}\mathrm{d}\overleftarrow{w}_{t},\quad x_{1}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The arrow indicates the direction of the process.
Therefore, once we define the forward SDE, we only need to find the score function to reverse it.
In practice, we train a network <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\theta}(x_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\theta}(x_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math> to approximate the score function by denoising score matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vincent, 2011</a>, DSM, )</cite>:</p>
<table id="A3.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{x\sim p_{\text{data}},\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)}\left[\lambda(t)\|s_{\theta}(x_{t},t)-\nabla\log p(x_{t}|x_{0}=x)\|^{2}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{x\sim p_{\text{data}},\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)}\left[\lambda(t)\|s_{\theta}(x_{t},t)-\nabla\log p(x_{t}|x_{0}=x)\|^{2}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is an optional weighting function, and <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}|x_{0}=x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}|x_{0}=x</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by the forward SDE.
In fact, the forward SDE can be written as a Gaussian kernel:</p>
<table id="A3.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p(x_{t}|x_{0}=x)=\mathcal{N}(x_{t}|m_{t}(x),v_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p(x_{t}|x_{0}=x)=\mathcal{N}(x_{t}|m_{t}(x),v_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle m_{t}(x)=\alpha_{t}x,\quad\alpha_{t}=\exp(\int_{0}^{t}\beta_{s}\mathrm{d}s),\quad v_{t}=\alpha_{t}^{2}\int_{0}^{t}\frac{\sigma_{s}^{2}}{\alpha_{s}^{2}}\mathrm{d}s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle m_{t}(x)=\alpha_{t}x,\quad\alpha_{t}=\exp(\int_{0}^{t}\beta_{s}\mathrm{d}s),\quad v_{t}=\alpha_{t}^{2}\int_{0}^{t}\frac{\sigma_{s}^{2}}{\alpha_{s}^{2}}\mathrm{d}s</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Hence, when training with DSM, we can directly calculate <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}=\alpha_{t}x+\sqrt{v_{t}}\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}=\alpha_{t}x+\sqrt{v_{t}}\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\log p(x_{t}|x_{0}=x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\log p(x_{t}|x_{0}=x)</annotation></semantics></math> is also analytically defined as the Gaussian kernel.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Flow matching.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Flow matching seeks to find a vector field <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math> that transport between the data and the Gaussian noise<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              Flow matching can be broadly defined between any two distributions, with arbitrary couplings. However, for simplicity, this section discuss the flow matching between Gaussian noise and data distribution, and assume the coupling between them is dependent.</span></span></span>.
The vector field defines an ODE:</p>
<table id="A3.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{d}x_{t}=v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)\mathrm{d}t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{d}x_{t}=v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)\mathrm{d}t</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Note that, unlike the SDE that needs different formulation for te forward and backward process, the ODE is already bidirectional.
To train the vector field, the most commonly choice is the conditional flow matching objective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lipman et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
Concretely, we first construct an interpolant between <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>,
denoted by <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then mininze</p>
<table id="A3.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{x\sim p_{\text{data}},\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)}\left[\|v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)-\partial_{t}x_{t}\|^{2}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{x\sim p_{\text{data}},\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)}\left[\|v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)-\partial_{t}x_{t}\|^{2}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">For the mostly used linear interpolant, we have</p>
<table id="A3.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{t}=(1-t)x_{0}+t\epsilon,\quad\partial_{t}x_{t}=\epsilon-x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.000em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{t}=(1-t)x_{0}+t\epsilon,\quad\partial_{t}x_{t}=\epsilon-x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Conversion between ODE and SDE</h4>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assuming accessing the score function, one can convert the ODE to the SDE.
Assume the marginal density for the above ODE is <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, then, the following two SDE has the same marginal density as the ODE:</p>
<table id="A3.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E21.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{d}x_{t}=\left(v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)+\frac{1}{2}\sigma^{2}_{t}\nabla\log p_{t}(x_{t})\right)\mathrm{d}t+\sigma_{t}\mathrm{d}\overrightarrow{w}_{t},\quad x_{0}\sim p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>→</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{d}x_{t}=\left(v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)+\frac{1}{2}\sigma^{2}_{t}\nabla\log p_{t}(x_{t})\right)\mathrm{d}t+\sigma_{t}\mathrm{d}\overrightarrow{w}_{t},\quad x_{0}\sim p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.E22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E22.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{d}x_{t}=\left(v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)-\frac{1}{2}\sigma^{2}_{t}\nabla\log p_{t}(x_{t})\right)\mathrm{d}t+\sigma_{t}\mathrm{d}\overleftarrow{w}_{t},\quad x_{1}\sim p_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>←</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{d}x_{t}=\left(v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)-\frac{1}{2}\sigma^{2}_{t}\nabla\log p_{t}(x_{t})\right)\mathrm{d}t+\sigma_{t}\mathrm{d}\overleftarrow{w}_{t},\quad x_{1}\sim p_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This is valid for any <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
There are models directly training the vector field and score separately, known as stochastic interpolant <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Albergo et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Equivalent between flow matching and diffusion model.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let’s recall the flow matching objective and the DSM objective, one can realize that the learned vector field and the score network are both a linear function of <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[x_{0}|x_{t}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[x_{0}|x_{t}]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, one can derive the equivalence between the vector field and the score function.
Taking the linear interpolant <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}=(1-t)x_{0}+t\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}=(1-t)x_{0}+t\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> as an example, when the vector field is trained to the optimal solution, we have</p>
<table id="A3.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla\log p_{t}(x_{t})=-\frac{x_{t}+(1-t)v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)}{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla\log p_{t}(x_{t})=-\frac{x_{t}+(1-t)v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)}{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Therefore, we can write <a href="#A3.E21" title="In Conversion between ODE and SDE ‣ C.3 Diffusion and Flow Matching ‣ Appendix C Additional Backgrounds and Related Works ‣ Compression as Adaptation:Implicit Visual Representation with Diffusion Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equations</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a> and <a href="#A3.E22" title="Equation 22 ‣ Conversion between ODE and SDE ‣ C.3 Diffusion and Flow Matching ‣ Appendix C Additional Backgrounds and Related Works ‣ Compression as Adaptation:Implicit Visual Representation with Diffusion Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a> into SDEs where the drifts are only given by the score function and <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Further, recall that we can choose <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#A3.E21" title="In Conversion between ODE and SDE ‣ C.3 Diffusion and Flow Matching ‣ Appendix C Additional Backgrounds and Related Works ‣ Compression as Adaptation:Implicit Visual Representation with Diffusion Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equations</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a> and <a href="#A3.E22" title="Equation 22 ‣ Conversion between ODE and SDE ‣ C.3 Diffusion and Flow Matching ‣ Appendix C Additional Backgrounds and Related Works ‣ Compression as Adaptation:Implicit Visual Representation with Diffusion Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a> freely.
Therefore, we can set it so that the drift of the forward SDE in <a href="#A3.E21" title="In Conversion between ODE and SDE ‣ C.3 Diffusion and Flow Matching ‣ Appendix C Additional Backgrounds and Related Works ‣ Compression as Adaptation:Implicit Visual Representation with Diffusion Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a> does not contains the score function.
Precisely,</p>
<table id="A3.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sigma_{t}=\sqrt{2t/(1-t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sigma_{t}=\sqrt{2t/(1-t)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Then, the forward SDE will become</p>
<table id="A3.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{d}x_{t}=-\frac{1}{1-t}x_{t}\mathrm{d}t+\sqrt{\frac{2t}{1-t}}\mathrm{d}\overrightarrow{w}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>→</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{d}x_{t}=-\frac{1}{1-t}x_{t}\mathrm{d}t+\sqrt{\frac{2t}{1-t}}\mathrm{d}\overrightarrow{w}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2.3" class="ltx_p">In other word, a flow matching trained with linear interpolant and independent coupling, is equivalent to a diffusion model defined via the forward SDE in <a href="#A3.E13" title="In Diffusion models. ‣ C.3 Diffusion and Flow Matching ‣ Appendix C Additional Backgrounds and Related Works ‣ Compression as Adaptation:Implicit Visual Representation with Diffusion Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>, where</p>
<table id="A3.EGx20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\beta_{t}=-\frac{1}{1-t},\quad\sigma_{t}=\sqrt{\frac{2t}{1-t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\beta_{t}=-\frac{1}{1-t},\quad\sigma_{t}=\sqrt{\frac{2t}{1-t}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.4 </span>Inference-time Scaling of Diffusion Models</h3>

<div id="A3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Inference-time scaling aims to spend more compute in inference time to improve or steer the generation process of the diffusion models.
A common framework is, given a pretrained diffusion model and a verifier, one runs several trajectories, or branch the trajectory with multiple particles, and then select the best particle according to the verifier.
The framework that is the most relevant to our approach is those based on Sequential Monte Carlo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, also known as Feynman-Kac method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Singhal et al., 2025</a>)</cite> in a broader sense.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These approaches aim to to tilt the diffusion process towards a reward function.
Specifically, for a pretrained diffusion model with denoising kernel <math id="A3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x_{t_{n-1}}|x_{t_{n}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x_{t_{n-1}}|x_{t_{n}})</annotation></semantics></math>, assuming its final sample follow a distribution with density <math id="A3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, these approaches aim to draw sample from <math id="A3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}(x)\exp(r(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}(x)\exp(r(x))</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is a reward function, or a likelihood in a inverse problem.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">First, these approaches define a proposal process, where the transition kernel is given by <math id="A3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(x_{t_{n-1}}|x_{t_{n}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(x_{t_{n-1}}|x_{t_{n}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
This process can be chosen to be the same as the pretrained diffusion, or can be guided by some guidance, similar to DPS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chung et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
Then, they define a sequence of intermediate reward functions <math id="A3.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This can be selected to be the final reward <math id="A3.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> evaluated on <math id="A3.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[x_{0}|x_{t_{n}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[x_{0}|x_{t_{n}}]</annotation></semantics></math>,
obtained following the Tweedie’s formula <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Efron, 2011</a>)</cite>, i.e.,</p>
<table id="A3.EGx21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r_{t_{n}}(x_{t})=\gamma_{t_{n}}r(\mathbb{E}[x_{0}|x_{t_{n}}])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r_{t_{n}}(x_{t})=\gamma_{t_{n}}r(\mathbb{E}[x_{0}|x_{t_{n}}])</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{t_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{t_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a scaling factor and typically <math id="A3.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{1}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A3.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, the SMC weight for each step is defined as</p>
<table id="A3.EGx22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle w\propto\frac{p(x_{t_{n-1}}|x_{t_{n}})\exp(r_{t_{n-1}}(x_{t_{n-1}}))}{q(x_{t_{n-1}}|x_{t_{n}})\exp(r_{t_{n}}(x_{t_{n}}))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∝</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle w\propto\frac{p(x_{t_{n-1}}|x_{t_{n}})\exp(r_{t_{n-1}}(x_{t_{n-1}}))}{q(x_{t_{n-1}}|x_{t_{n}})\exp(r_{t_{n}}(x_{t_{n}}))}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS4.p3.3" class="ltx_p">More broadly, similar SMC algorithm can be used for inference-time annealing, product, or CFG debiasing, for both Gaussian diffusion models and discrete diffusions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Skreta et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">From this perspective, our proposed scaling algorithm takes a trivial reward function <math id="A3.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0</annotation></semantics></math>, use the adapted diffusion model as the prior, and take the analytical kernel in <a href="#S3.E9" title="In Inference-time scaling with sampling ‣ 3.3 Inference-Time Scaling and Control ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Compression as Adaptation:Implicit Visual Representation with Diffusion Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> as the target.
In principle, we could further guide the generation by introducing a reward and performing reward-guided selection. However, doing so would substantially increase the number of function evaluations (NFE).
In contrast, our current algorithm introduces no additional functional calls:
it only adds Gaussian-kernel sampling and evaluation, which is lightweight relatively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.5 </span>Reverse Channel Coding and Relative Entropy Coding</h3>

<div id="A3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reverse Channel Coding, also known as Relative Entropy Coding, aims to encode a random sample from a target distribution <math id="A3.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, using a shared prior distribution <math id="A3.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and the shared PRNG.
For a more comprehensive introduction and explanation, we refer the reader to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Theis &amp; Ahmed, 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The most intuitive approach for performing relative entropy coding is minimal random coding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Havasi et al., 2019</a>, MRC, )</cite>, where the encoder draws <math id="A3.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> samples from <math id="A3.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> using the shared PRNG, and transmit the index of the selected sample to the decoder.
Here, to ensure the sample has small bias, <math id="A3.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is typically set to <math id="A3.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\exp(D_{\text{KL}}[q||p])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp(D_{\text{KL}}[q||p])</annotation></semantics></math>, and encoding the index costs roughly <math id="A3.SS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\text{KL}}[q||p]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{KL}}[q||p]</annotation></semantics></math> nats.
Several following approaches improve the coding efficiency of MRC.
Particular, ordered random coding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Theis &amp; Ahmed, 2022</a>, ORC, )</cite> added a permuted Gumbel noise to ensure that we not only choose the best sample, but also choose the one with the smallest index.
A* Coding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Flamich et al., 2022</a>)</cite> and Greedy Poisson rejection sampling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Flamich, 2023</a>, GPRS, )</cite> utilize Poisson process to control the bitrate in a similar way as ORC.
Due to the Poisson process, ORC, A* Coding and GPRS could achieve a finite codelength even when for infinite number of candidates drawn from <math id="A3.SS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>.
One can show that the theoretical codelength of these approaches is bounded by</p>
<table id="A3.EGx23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E29.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle D{{}_{\text{KL}}}[q||p]+\log_{2}(D{{}_{\text{KL}}}[q||p]+1)+\text{const}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mtext>KL</mtext><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mtext>KL</mtext><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mtext>const</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D{{}_{\text{KL}}}[q||p]+\log_{2}(D{{}_{\text{KL}}}[q||p]+1)+\text{const}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the KL is in bits.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, Relative Entropy Coding is challenging.
To ensure a small bias, these approaches all requires <math id="A3.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\exp(D_{\text{KL}}[q||p]))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\exp(D_{\text{KL}}[q||p]))</annotation></semantics></math> or even <math id="A3.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\exp(D_{\infty}[q||p]))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\exp(D_{\infty}[q||p]))</annotation></semantics></math> number of candidate samples.
While some approaches aim to accelerate the algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Flamich et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Flamich, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, they are still limited by strong assumptions on the distribution.
Therefore, these algorithms are still constraint in practice.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Fortunately, in our proposed inference-time scaling algorithm, the Relative Entropy Coding component is only used to refine the performance of the basic compression via adaption.
Therefore, even when the KL divergence between the target and proposal is large, we do not need an exponentially large number of particles.
In other word, we use the Relative Entropy Coding algorithm only as a lightweight refinement component, significantly reducing its complexity.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.6 </span>Lossy Compression with Relative Entropy Coding and Diffusion</h3>

<div id="A3.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As discussed in the main text, our scaling algorithm can also be interpreted through the lens of
Diff-C <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Theis et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, a lossy compression framework based on diffusion models and
relative entropy coding.
A key difference lies in computation required for Diff-C and our scaling algorithm.
Diff-C uses an unadapted, generic diffusion model to define the proposal denoising kernel <math id="A3.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x_{t_{n-1}}|x_{t_{n}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x_{t_{n-1}}|x_{t_{n}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
As a result, the number of candidates required for effective selection can become prohibitive—often scaling exponentially with the KL divergence between the target and the proposal.
In contrast, our adapted model is already close to the desired sample, so importance sampling serves as a further refinement.
Empirically, using only <math id="A3.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>10</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{10}</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="A3.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{18}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>18</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{18}</annotation></semantics></math> candidates per step already yields substantial gains.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally, Diff-C therefore encodes only the first <math id="A3.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{\prime}&lt;N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{\prime}&lt;N</annotation></semantics></math> diffusion steps.
The remaining steps are directly generated by the pretrained diffusion.
To control the bitrate, Diff-C adjust <math id="A3.SS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In contrast, our approach controls the bitrate primarily through the entropy model applied to the adaptation parameters.
The inference-time scaling component contributes only a negligible overhead—approximately
<math id="A3.SS6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.00005" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.00005</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.00005</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="A3.SS6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.0005" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.0005</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.0005</annotation></semantics></math> bpp for our 480p video clips, and therefore we do not truncate the process as in
Diff-C; instead, we apply it over the entire generation trajectory.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vonderfecht &amp; Liu (2025)</a></cite> introduced a CUDA-accelerated implementation of
Diff-C, making Diff-C practical for the first time.
Although our scaling algorithm is not as computationally demanding as Diff-C, it can
also benefit from their implementation for further acceleration.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Proposition and Proofs</h2>

<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Justification of the Overfitting Objective</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we show that our training objective in <a href="#S3.E1" title="In Training objective ‣ 3.1 Learning Implicit Visual Representations ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Compression as Adaptation:Implicit Visual Representation with Diffusion Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> from the perspective of MDL, assuming the diffusion process.
For easier reference, we will repeat key equations below.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The generation process of a pretrained diffusion model is written as</p>
<table id="A4.EGx24" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.E30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E30.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{d}x_{t}=\Big(\beta_{t}x_{t}-\sigma_{t}^{2}\nabla\log p_{t}^{\text{data}}(x_{t})\Big)\,\mathrm{d}t+\sigma_{t}\,\mathrm{d}\overleftarrow{w}_{t},\qquad x_{1}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.170em">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>←</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{d}x_{t}=\Big(\beta_{t}x_{t}-\sigma_{t}^{2}\nabla\log p_{t}^{\text{data}}(x_{t})\Big)\,\mathrm{d}t+\sigma_{t}\,\mathrm{d}\overleftarrow{w}_{t},\qquad x_{1}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}^{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}^{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the density of the data distribution convolved with the Gaussian noise at time <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., the marginal distribution of <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> under the forward noising process).
Under the flow-matching parameterization, one has <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\beta_{t}=-1/(1-t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}=-1/(1-t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}=\sqrt{2t/(1-t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}=\sqrt{2t/(1-t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the score function is directly related to the learned vector field.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This SDE induces a path space together with a probability measure, which we denote by <math id="A4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℙ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Our goal is to construct a new measure <math id="A4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> that reconstruct <math id="A4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="A4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math> while introducing as little additional information as possible relative to <math id="A4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℙ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The information-minimizing solution is the posterior of <math id="A4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℙ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}</annotation></semantics></math> given the terminal event <math id="A4.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}=x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}=x</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be characterized via Doob’s <math id="A4.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-transform.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Concretely, conditioning the base dynamics on the event <math id="A4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}\in\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}\in\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> yields the controlled reverse-time SDE</p>
<table id="A4.EGx25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.E31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E31.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{d}x_{t}=\Big(\beta_{t}x_{t}-\sigma_{t}^{2}\nabla\log p_{t}^{\text{data}}(x_{t})-\sigma_{t}^{2}\nabla\log h_{t}(x_{t})\Big)\,\mathrm{d}t+\sigma_{t}\,\mathrm{d}\overleftarrow{w}_{t},\qquad x_{1}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.170em">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>←</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{d}x_{t}=\Big(\beta_{t}x_{t}-\sigma_{t}^{2}\nabla\log p_{t}^{\text{data}}(x_{t})-\sigma_{t}^{2}\nabla\log h_{t}(x_{t})\Big)\,\mathrm{d}t+\sigma_{t}\,\mathrm{d}\overleftarrow{w}_{t},\qquad x_{1}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="A4.EGx26" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.E32"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle h_{t}(x_{t})=\mathbb{P}\{x_{0}\in\mathcal{A}\mid x_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle h_{t}(x_{t})=\mathbb{P}\{x_{0}\in\mathcal{A}\mid x_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">is the probability of hitting <math id="A4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="A4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math> when starting from <math id="A4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and following to the base dynamics.
In the special case <math id="A4.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}=\{x\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}=\{x\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{t}(x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{t}(x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> reduces to the conditional terminal density <math id="A4.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x_{0}=x|x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x_{0}=x|x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let’s look at the term <math id="A4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-\sigma_{t}^{2}\nabla\log p_{t}^{\text{data}}(x_{t})-\sigma_{t}^{2}\nabla\log h_{t}(x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\sigma_{t}^{2}\nabla\log p_{t}^{\text{data}}(x_{t})-\sigma_{t}^{2}\nabla\log h_{t}(x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, this can be simplified as</p>
<table id="A4.EGx27" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.E33"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E33.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\sigma_{t}^{2}\nabla\log p_{t}^{\text{data}}(x_{t})+\sigma_{t}^{2}\nabla\log h_{t}(x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sigma_{t}^{2}\nabla\log p_{t}^{\text{data}}(x_{t})+\sigma_{t}^{2}\nabla\log h_{t}(x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.E34"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E34.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\sigma_{t}^{2}\nabla(\log p(x_{0}=x|x_{t})+\log p^{\text{data}}_{t}(x_{t}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>log</mi><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sigma_{t}^{2}\nabla(\log p(x_{0}=x|x_{t})+\log p^{\text{data}}_{t}(x_{t}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(34)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.E35"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E35.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\sigma_{t}^{2}\nabla(\log p(x_{t}|x_{0}=x)+p^{\text{data}}_{0}(x_{0}=x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn><mtext>data</mtext></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sigma_{t}^{2}\nabla(\log p(x_{t}|x_{0}=x)+p^{\text{data}}_{0}(x_{0}=x))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(35)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.E36"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E36.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\sigma_{t}^{2}\nabla\log p(x_{t}|x_{0}=x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sigma_{t}^{2}\nabla\log p(x_{t}|x_{0}=x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(36)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">The following equality holds as <math id="A4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∇</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla</annotation></semantics></math> is taken only over <math id="A4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the last line holds when the pretrained diffusion perfectly reverse the noising forward process, which is a common assumption we can take.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, notice that <math id="A4.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x_{t}|x_{0}=x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x_{t}|x_{0}=x)</annotation></semantics></math> is a simple Gaussian kernel, defined in <a href="#A3.E16" title="In Diffusion models. ‣ C.3 Diffusion and Flow Matching ‣ Appendix C Additional Backgrounds and Related Works ‣ Compression as Adaptation:Implicit Visual Representation with Diffusion Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>.
Under the flow matching setup, this kernel is given by</p>
<table id="A4.EGx28" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.E37"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p(x_{t}|x_{0}=x)=\mathcal{N}(x_{t}|(1-t)x_{0},t^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p(x_{t}|x_{0}=x)=\mathcal{N}(x_{t}|(1-t)x_{0},t^{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(37)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Hence, the optimal vector field satisfies</p>
<table id="A4.EGx29" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.E38"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-\frac{(x_{t}-(1-t)x)}{t^{2}}=-\frac{x_{t}+(1-t)v^{*}}{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\frac{(x_{t}-(1-t)x)}{t^{2}}=-\frac{x_{t}+(1-t)v^{*}}{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(38)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.p6.3" class="ltx_p">Considering <math id="A4.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}=(1-t)x+t\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}=(1-t)x+t\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
<table id="A4.EGx30" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.E39"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v^{*}=\epsilon-x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v^{*}=\epsilon-x</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(39)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.p6.4" class="ltx_p">This also coincides with the optimal vector field between a delta distribution at <math id="A4.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and Gaussian.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>Bounds on the Distortion of Early Stopping</h3>

<div id="A4.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proof has three parts. We first analyze the error introduced by the one-step map, and then we will analyze the error introduced by the integrating the vector field with error.
Finally, we will put the error together, obtaining an upper bound on the overall error.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">(1) Error introduced by one-step map.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We assume <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}=x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}=x</annotation></semantics></math> is the data to be compressed.
We train a vector field, and the optimal solution is given by <a href="#S3.E8" title="In Inference-time scaling with sampling ‣ 3.3 Inference-Time Scaling and Control ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Compression as Adaptation:Implicit Visual Representation with Diffusion Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>:</p>
<table id="A4.EGx31" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.E40"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v^{*}(x_{t},t)={(x_{t}-x)}/{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v^{*}(x_{t},t)={(x_{t}-x)}/{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(40)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Therefore, the one-step prediction of <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> (which we denoted as <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math>) from <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="A4.EGx32" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.E41"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{x}=\tilde{x}_{\tau}-\tau v_{\theta}(\tilde{x}_{\tau},\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{x}=\tilde{x}_{\tau}-\tau v_{\theta}(\tilde{x}_{\tau},\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(41)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Note that we also take the error in <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> into account.
We can hence calculate the reconstruction error as</p>
<table id="A4.EGx33" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.E42"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\delta</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E42.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\tilde{x}-x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\tilde{x}-x</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(42)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.E43"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\tilde{x}_{\tau}-\tau v_{\theta}(\tilde{x}_{\tau},\tau)-x_{\tau}+\tau v^{*}(x_{\tau},\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\tilde{x}_{\tau}-\tau v_{\theta}(\tilde{x}_{\tau},\tau)-x_{\tau}+\tau v^{*}(x_{\tau},\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(43)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.E44"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\delta_{\tau}-\tau(v_{\theta}(\tilde{x}_{\tau},\tau)-v^{*}(x_{\tau},\tau))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\delta_{\tau}-\tau(v_{\theta}(\tilde{x}_{\tau},\tau)-v^{*}(x_{\tau},\tau))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(44)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.E45"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\delta_{\tau}-\tau(v_{\theta}(\tilde{x}_{\tau},\tau)-(x_{1}-x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\delta_{\tau}-\tau(v_{\theta}(\tilde{x}_{\tau},\tau)-(x_{1}-x))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(45)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the error at step <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">(2) Error introduced by error in the vector field.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now, let’s look at how <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated.
We start from <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and follow the ODE backwards in time:</p>
<table id="A4.EGx34" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.E46"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\mathrm{d}\tilde{x}_{t}}{\mathrm{d}t}=v_{\theta}(\tilde{x}_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\mathrm{d}\tilde{x}_{t}}{\mathrm{d}t}=v_{\theta}(\tilde{x}_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(46)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and recall the ground truth path is also known:</p>
<table id="A4.EGx35" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.E47"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\mathrm{d}{x}_{t}}{\mathrm{d}t}=v^{*}({x}_{t},t)=x_{1}-x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\mathrm{d}{x}_{t}}{\mathrm{d}t}=v^{*}({x}_{t},t)=x_{1}-x</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(47)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">We can hence calculate the error</p>
<table id="A4.EGx36" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.E48"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\mathrm{d}\delta_{t}}{\mathrm{d}t}=v_{\theta}(\tilde{x}_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x),\quad\delta_{1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\mathrm{d}\delta_{t}}{\mathrm{d}t}=v_{\theta}(\tilde{x}_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x),\quad\delta_{1}=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(48)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">and therefore,</p>
<table id="A4.EGx37" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.E49"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\delta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\delta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E49.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\int_{1}^{\tau}(v_{\theta}(\tilde{x}_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x))\mathrm{d}t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>τ</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int_{1}^{\tau}(v_{\theta}(\tilde{x}_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x))\mathrm{d}t</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(49)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.E50"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E50.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-\int_{\tau}^{1}(v_{\theta}(\tilde{x}_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x))\mathrm{d}t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\int_{\tau}^{1}(v_{\theta}(\tilde{x}_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x))\mathrm{d}t</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(50)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">(3) Putting them together. </h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Putting the errors together, we obtain</p>
<table id="A4.EGx38" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.E51"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|\delta\|=\|\int_{\tau}^{1}(v_{\theta}(\tilde{x}_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x))\mathrm{d}t+\tau(v_{\theta}(\tilde{x}_{\tau},\tau)-(x_{1}-x))\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|\delta\|=\|\int_{\tau}^{1}(v_{\theta}(\tilde{x}_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x))\mathrm{d}t+\tau(v_{\theta}(\tilde{x}_{\tau},\tau)-(x_{1}-x))\|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(51)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Now, introduce the error of the learned vector field:</p>
<table id="A4.EGx39" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.E52"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle e_{t}=v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle e_{t}=v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(52)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Note that the error is introduced on the clean path <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, we have</p>
<table id="A4.EGx40" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.E53"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|v_{\theta}(\tilde{x}_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x)\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|v_{\theta}(\tilde{x}_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x)\|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E53.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\|v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x)+v_{\theta}(\tilde{x}_{t},t)-v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\|v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x)+v_{\theta}(\tilde{x}_{t},t)-v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)\|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(53)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.E54"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\|v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x)\|+\|v_{\theta}(\tilde{x}_{t},t)-v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\|v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x)\|+\|v_{\theta}(\tilde{x}_{t},t)-v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)\|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(54)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">As we further assume the learned network is <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz continuous, hence</p>
<table id="A4.EGx41" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.E55"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|v_{\theta}(\tilde{x}_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x)\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|v_{\theta}(\tilde{x}_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x)\|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E55.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\|v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x)\|+L\|\tilde{x}_{t}-x_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\|v_{\theta}(x_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x)\|+L\|\tilde{x}_{t}-x_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(55)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.E56"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\|e_{t}\|+L\|\delta_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\|e_{t}\|+L\|\delta_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(56)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.5" class="ltx_p">Therefore, we have</p>
<table id="A4.EGx42" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.E57"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|\delta_{\tau}\|\leq\int_{\tau}^{1}\|v_{\theta}(\tilde{x}_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x)\|\mathrm{d}t\leq\int_{\tau}^{1}(\|e_{t}\|+L\|\delta_{t}\|)\mathrm{d}t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|\delta_{\tau}\|\leq\int_{\tau}^{1}\|v_{\theta}(\tilde{x}_{t},t)-(x_{1}-x)\|\mathrm{d}t\leq\int_{\tau}^{1}(\|e_{t}\|+L\|\delta_{t}\|)\mathrm{d}t</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(57)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.6" class="ltx_p">With Grönwall’s inequality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gronwall, 1919</a>)</cite>, we have</p>
<table id="A4.EGx43" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.E58"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|\delta_{\tau}\|\leq\exp(L(1-\tau))\int_{\tau}^{1}\|e_{t}\|\mathrm{d}t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|\delta_{\tau}\|\leq\exp(L(1-\tau))\int_{\tau}^{1}\|e_{t}\|\mathrm{d}t</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(58)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.7" class="ltx_p">Therefore, we have</p>
<table id="A4.EGx44" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.E59"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|\delta\|\leq(\tau L+1)\|\delta_{\tau}\|+\tau\|e_{\tau}\|\leq(\tau L+1)\exp(L(1-\tau))\int_{\tau}^{1}\|e_{t}\|\mathrm{d}t+\tau\|e_{\tau}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|\delta\|\leq(\tau L+1)\|\delta_{\tau}\|+\tau\|e_{\tau}\|\leq(\tau L+1)\exp(L(1-\tau))\int_{\tau}^{1}\|e_{t}\|\mathrm{d}t+\tau\|e_{\tau}\|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(59)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.8" class="ltx_p">∎</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Experimental Details</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For image experiments, we use <span id="A5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen-Image</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> as our base model; and for video experiments, we use <span id="A5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Wan2.1-T2V-1.3B-Diffusers</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wan et al., 2025</a>)</cite> as our base model.
We now describe additional experimental settings below.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Representation Experiments</h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The detailed settings for experiments of image and video representations are described in Table <a href="#A5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ E.1 Representation Experiments ‣ Appendix E Experimental Details ‣ Compression as Adaptation:Implicit Visual Representation with Diffusion Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#A5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ E.1 Representation Experiments ‣ Appendix E Experimental Details ‣ Compression as Adaptation:Implicit Visual Representation with Diffusion Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.SS1.tab1" class="ltx_table">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure ltx_flex_table">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A5.T1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:165.6pt;">
